@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the searches of the Typhonian run of 2026-10-21
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason given in prima-materia#703.
# Three searches: one for the receiving organ across the reachable Typhonian
# volumes, one for the microtubule mapping across every reachable work the
# circulating sentence names a party to, and one recording which works of
# the corpus could not be reached and by which ways.
#

pmp:TyphonianNervusAntennaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Typhonian corpus names the nervous system as the organ that receives non-human intelligence"@en, "Ob der typhonische Bestand das Nervensystem als Organ nennt, das nicht-menschliche Intelligenz empfängt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Grant, The Magical Revival (1972, in the reprint named at the tradition instance), Cults of the Shadow (1975), Nightside of Eden (1977, Skoob 1994), Outside the Circles of Time (1980), Outer Gateways (1994), Beyond the Mauve Zone (1999) and The Ninth Arch (2002), all seven searched whole by term in their full-text layers; The Magical Revival and Outside the Circles of Time read at the wording at every hit."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which this transmission makes the nervous system, the brain or any physiological organ the receiver of extraterrestrial or extradimensional forces, in the manner the circulating sentence of prima-materia#888 credits to Grant; and, as its negation, a passage placing the receiving organ outside physiology."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search, case-insensitive, for: nervous system, nervous structure, nerve, ganglia, brain, cerebral, antenna, antennae, aerial, receiver, receiving set, receiving station, receiving apparatus, wireless, radio, transmitter, endocrine, chakra, extra-physical, extra-terrestrial, praeter-human, praeterhuman. Counts across the seven volumes: nervous system four, antenna or antennae seventeen, receiver or receiving apparatus five, wireless or radio twenty-two. Every hit in the two opened works read in its place; the hits in the five others read in their immediate context only."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundOpposing ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:TyphonianAntennaeReceptivae, pmc:TyphonianReceptorFortuneCitatus, pmc:TyphonianChakraExtraPhysica, pmc:TyphonianMensSineNervis ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "The outcome is opposing material with one near find, and both are entered. The near find is the antenna image of Outside the Circles of Time, which belongs to the astral consciousness and to the artist as a sensitive machine, leaves the sender open between outer and inner space, and does not name the nervous system. The opposing material is in the first volume: the chakras are placed in extra-physical dimensions beyond physical investigation, and mind is said to exist independently of brain or nervous structure. The one wireless receiver in the corpus is a sentence quoted from Dion Fortune. Of the four hits for nervous system across seven volumes, one is a physiological aside on sexual abstinence, one calls the gods companions responding to evocations of the nervous system, one qualifies the tunnels of the nightside as the ganglia of an occult nervous system, and one describes a dead man's paralysis."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search did not settle. Five of the seven volumes were searched by term and not read, so a receiver statement phrased without any of the listed terms would have escaped it; and three further works of the corpus could not be reached at all, as the third search below records. The negative half of this finding is therefore relative to twenty-two terms and seven volumes, not to the transmission."@en .

pmp:TyphonianMicrotubulusSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether any Typhonian or chaos-magical work maps the receiver model onto neural microtubules"@en, "Ob irgendein typhonisches oder chaosmagisches Werk das Empfängermodell auf neuronale Mikrotubuli abbildet"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The seven Typhonian volumes named in the first search; Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Weiser, 1987), Liber Kaos (in the revised Weiser edition of 2023) and The Apophenion (2008); Leary, Starseed (1973) and Exo-Psychology (1977); Wilson, Cosmic Trigger I (1977, in the re-edition of 1986); all searched whole by term in their full-text layers."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage in which a Typhonian or chaos-magical author maps contact with non-human intelligence, or any receiver model, onto the quantum properties of neural microtubules, or names the orchestrated objective reduction theory of Penrose and Hameroff; the second clause of the sentence held at prima-materia#888."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search, case-insensitive, for: microtubule, microtubules, tubulin, Penrose, Hameroff, Orch, orchestrated, objective reduction, quantum. Microtubule, tubulin, Hameroff and Orch: zero in every work. Penrose: two, both in The Apophenion, one a quoted sentence on the nature of time, one a bibliography entry for The Road to Reality. Quantum: fifty-six in Liber Kaos, eighty-seven in The Apophenion, forty-eight in Exo-Psychology, fifty-three in Cosmic Trigger, one each in Outside the Circles of Time and Beyond the Mauve Zone, zero in the other five Typhonian volumes; the quantum hits in the Typhonian volumes read in place and found to be the phrase quantum-leap and a single use in the poor text layer."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the nothing is bounded. The Typhonian volumes predate the theory they are said to have adopted, with the last of them two years after the second major statement of it; the chaos-magical works searched are three of one author, and the one that engages quantum physics at length, The Apophenion, names Penrose for time and never for microtubules. Authors not searched: Hine, Dukes, Fries, Bertiaux, Chumbley, Staley, the Starfire journal, and the physicists with an interest in the occult. The gap stands here so that whoever holds those works can close it."@en .

pmp:TyphonianOperaInaccessaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the remaining works of the Typhonian corpus can be reached as full text"@en, "Ob die übrigen Werke des typhonischen Korpus als Volltext erreichbar sind"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Grant, Aleister Crowley and the Hidden God (1973); Grant, Hecate's Fountain (1992); Grant, Images and Oracles of Austin Osman Spare (1975); Grant, Remembering Aleister Crowley (1991); Kenneth and Steffi Grant, Zos Speaks! (1998); Kenneth and Steffi Grant, Hidden Lore (1989); Grant, Manifesto of New Isis Lodge (1955); Grant, The LAM Statement (1987); sought as full texts for the harvest of the transmission."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A readable full-text layer of each work, in any edition, so that the claims the secondary literature reports from them could be read at the wording: among them the lodge as a gate in space for extraterrestrial energies, the Lam statement, and the manifesto of 1955 with its transplutonic planet."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "The open scan archive searched by title and author for each work, with the author in both name orders, on 2026-10-21. Found: nothing for the first five; for Hidden Lore one scan dated 1996 under a lending restriction with its full text refused; for the manifesto and the statement nothing. Not yet tried: other title transcriptions, the Starfire journal volume as a whole, a library with its own full-text access, a physical copy."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:corpusUnreachable ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "A property of availability and not of the route: the works are in copyright, most of them in print from Starfire or Fulgur, and the one copy found is lending-restricted, which the holding records as a block at the work. The other seven volumes of the trilogies lay in the same archive as open full texts without imprint or date in their metadata, and the two that were read are cited with the edition identified from their own text layers; a later reader should prefer a print and may find the reprint pagination to differ from the first edition for the Skoob volumes."@en .
